  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThrough Today: Continued dry conditions with cooler than normal \nmorning lows and slightly warmer than normal afternoon highs. \nSurface ridging will continue across the region from the northeast\nresulting in an east wind and keeping the Gulf closed through \nthis evening. Ridging aloft will remain over the region as well. \nTogether these features will result in decent radiational cooling \nand near full insolation. The result will be critically low \nafternoon minimum relative humidity maintaining fire danger \nconcerns. /22/\n\nTonight through Saturday...High pressure at the surface centered \nto our north and increasing ridging aloft from the west through \nthe week, will maintain quiet weather across the forecast area. \nThe overall dry air mass will remain across the region through the\nweek as dew points generally mix into the 30s & 40s each \nafternoon. However, temperatures will steadily warm each day. This\nwarming will be felt more with daytime highs, than with overnight\nlows. Lows through the period will generally range from the mid \n40s to mid 50s. Meanwhile highs on Tuesday will range from the \nupper 70s to middle 80s. Then by Saturday, they'll have increased \ninto the lower 80s to around 90.\n\nThese increasing high temperatures in this dry air mass will result \nin afternoon minimum relative humidity values falling to critically \nlow values, i.e. generally into the 20-30% range.  This, coupled \nwith the extremely dry soil conditions across the area will continue \nto promote at least a \"Limited Threat\" for dangerous fire conditions \nacross the forecast area through the period.  Please continue to \nmonitor the latest forecast and outlook through the week, as some \ncategorical adjustment could be made to portions of, if not all of, \nthe forecast area. /19/",
